🔥

🔴 6. Workspaces (Environment Management)
🧠 What is a Workspace?

👉 A workspace in Terraform lets you manage multiple environments using the same configuration

Examples:

dev
stage
prod

👉 Same code, different state files

🔹 Simple Understanding
One Terraform codebase -> multiple environments -> separate state per workspace

✅ Useful when you want:

Same infra pattern
Different environment names
Separate state tracking

🔥 Why Workspaces Are Important?
1. Environment Separation
Keep dev, stage, and prod isolated
2. Same Code Reuse
No need to duplicate Terraform files
3. Safer Deployments
Changes in dev do not affect prod
4. Better Team Workflow
Easy to test in non-production first

🔹 Default Workspace

Terraform starts with:

default

👉 If you do nothing, Terraform uses the `default` workspace

🔥 Important Workspace Commands
Create workspace
terraform workspace new dev

List workspaces
terraform workspace list

Switch workspace
terraform workspace select prod

Show current workspace
terraform workspace show

Delete workspace
terraform workspace delete stage

⚠️ You cannot delete the workspace you are currently using

🔄 How Workspaces Actually Work

When you switch workspace:

Terraform keeps separate state for each workspace

Example:

default -> default state
dev -> dev state
prod -> prod state

👉 This means resources created in `dev` are tracked separately from `prod`

🔥 Real Example
variable "env" {
  default = "dev"
}

resource "aws_instance" "demo" {
  ami           = "ami-0f5ee92e2d63afc18"
  instance_type = terraform.workspace == "prod" ? "t3.medium" : "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Environment = terraform.workspace
    Name        = "app-${terraform.workspace}"
  }
}

👉 If current workspace = `prod`
instance_type -> `t3.medium`

👉 If current workspace = `dev`
instance_type -> `t2.micro`

🔹 Built-in Variable

Terraform provides:

terraform.workspace

👉 It returns the current workspace name

Use cases:

Conditional resource sizing
Environment-based naming
Different tags per environment

⚠️ Important Limitation

Workspaces separate state
They do NOT create completely separate code folders

👉 If environments need very different configurations, separate folders or separate root modules may be better

🔥 When to Use Workspaces?

✅ Good for:

dev / test / prod with mostly same infra
Small to medium environment separation
Learning and interview scenarios

❌ Not ideal for:

Completely different architectures
Complex enterprise setups with many differences

🧠 Interview Questions
❓ What is a Terraform workspace?

👉
"Terraform workspaces allow us to use the same configuration for multiple environments by keeping separate state files."

❓ Why use workspaces?

👉 To manage `dev`, `stage`, and `prod` without duplicating code

❓ What does `terraform.workspace` do?

👉 It returns the currently selected workspace name

❓ Workspace vs separate folders?

👉 Workspaces are good for similar environments; separate folders are better when environments differ a lot

🔥 Real Interview Scenario
❓ How do you deploy the same infra to dev and prod?

👉
"I can use Terraform workspaces to switch between environments. Each workspace maintains separate state, and I can use `terraform.workspace` for environment-specific naming, tags, or instance sizes."

🧠 Best Practices
Use workspaces only when environments are mostly similar
Use clear naming like dev, stage, prod
Do not depend only on workspaces for very complex production environments
Combine workspaces with remote backend for team usage

✅ Quick Summary
Workspace = environment separation
Same code -> different state
Useful for dev/stage/prod
`terraform.workspace` gives current environment

